          "distinct": true,
          "id": "f470a65f8c9cc507491eb548671332d04adb065e",
          "message": "feat(generator-ts): generate typescript source code (#26779)\n\n# TypeScript client generator\n\nImplement the new ESM-compatible TypeScript client generator (Preview).\n\n## Breaking changes\n\n- The output is TypeScript source code. You need to use a modern JS\nruntime with native TypeScript support or transpile it.\n- `$DbEnums` enum in TypedSQL is generated as a single interface.\nPreviously it was split into an interface with invalid identifiers and a\nnamespace with valid identifiers, but we can't rely on declaration\nmerging of those anymore.\n- The entrypoint for TypedSQL is `sql.ts` and not `sql/index.ts`. An\n`index.ts` file for the client itself is still temporarily provided\n(re-exports everything from `client.ts`) but will be removed later.\nIndex files and directory imports don't exist in ESM (except when\nemulated by bundlers) and should not be used in modern ESM-first code.\n- Removed a bunch of obsolete types from `Prisma` namespace that were\nnot used anywhere. Removing them in the old client is technically a\nbreaking change but we can easily do it here.\n- `Prisma.dmmf` has been removed. Preserving the old behaviour was\ntricky in pure TS (previously we defined a getter on the the JS object\nthe shape of which was described by the TS namespace declaration) and\nevaluating it eagerly wasn't nice because it would be wasting CPU cycles\non something that most users won't need, so I dropped it for now. If\nnecessary, it can be re-introduced as a plain function (e.g.\n`Prisma.getDmmf()`) later.\n- New client doesn't not load `.env` files at run time. Use `dotenv`\nmanually or ensure the environment variables exist in any other way.\n\n## Limitations\n\n- Generated code still uses features such as namespaces (though some\nusages of those have already been eliminated, e.g., in TypedSQL), so\nit's not yet compatible with `erasableSyntaxOnly` (e.g. when running\nnatively on Node.js 22+ without the `--experimental-transform-types` CLI\nflag). However, it's fully compatible with other modern runtimes, `tsx`,\n`tsc` and bundlers.\n- There's no browser bundle anymore, and there's no way to safely import\ntypes or enums in frontend code yet.\n\nBoth of these issues will be resolved by the file splitting work.\n\n## Generator options\n\n```prisma\ngenerator client {\n  // Required\n  provider               = \"prisma-client-ts\"\n  output                 = \"../src/generated/prisma\"\n\n  // Optional\n  runtime                = \"nodejs\"\n  generatedFileExtension = \"ts\"\n  importFileExtension    = \"ts\"\n  moduleFormat           = \"esm\"\n}\n```\n\nOnly `output` is required. `runtime` defaults to `nodejs`,\n`generatedFileExtension` defaults to `ts`, and the rest of the options\nare automatically inferred from `tsconfig.json` and the values of other\noptions (though of course the inference isn't perfect and you may want\nto override those).\n\n### `runtime`\n\nTarget runtime to generate code for.\n\nAvailable options:\n- `nodejs` (alias `node`)\n- `deno`\n- `bun`\n- `deno-deploy`\n- `workerd` (alias `cloudflare`)\n- `edge-light` (alias `vercel`)\n- `react-native`\n\n### `moduleFormat`\n\nModule format to generate code for. Necessary so we know if we can use\n`import.meta.url` or `__dirname`.\n\nAvailable options:\n- `cjs` (alias `commonjs`)\n- `esm`\n\n### `generatedFileExtension`\n\nThe file extension to use in generated TypeScript files (i.e., `ts`,\n`mts` or `cts`).\n\n### `importFileExtension`\n\nThe file extension to use in import statements. You can provide an\nextension (such as `ts`, `mts`, `cts`, `js`, `mjs`, `cjs`) or an empty\nstring to use bare module specifiers.\n\nModern runtimes with native typescript support either require (Deno,\nNode.js 22) or support (Bun) importing TypeScript files using the same\nextension that your source files have (e.g. `import { PrismaClient }\nfrom './client.ts'`). It is also supported by `tsx` and `ts-node`.\n\nWhen transpiling to JavaScript with ESM, normally you must use the\ntarget extension of the not-yet-transpiled JavaScript files in import\nstatements in TypeScript (e.g. `import { PrismaClient } from\n'./client.js'`, even though there's no `client.js` file on disk in the\nsource directory), unless you use the new\n`rewriteRelativeImportExtensions` option introduced in TypeScript 5.7,\nwhich allows you to use the `ts` extension in this case.\n\nWhen transpiling to CommonJS, you can use either `js` or no extension at\nall (so called bare module specifiers, e.g. `import { PrismaClient }\nfrom './client'`).\n\nWhen using a bundler, you can typically use whatever, and the bundler\nwill figure it out anyhow. Bun is also very forgiving and behaves\nsimilarly to bundlers in terms of module resolution.\n\nWe try to infer a reasonable default based on these facts, being biased\ntowards modern runtimes and patterns.\n\n## Next steps\n\n- Run tests with different runtime targets\n- File splitting\n- Ecosystem tests and e2e tests\n\n---\n\n/integration\n\nCloses:\nhttps://linear.app/prisma-company/issue/ORM-813/generate-typescript-client\nCloses:\nhttps://linear.app/prisma-company/issue/ORM-689/implement-new-typescript-client-generator",
          "timestamp": "2025-04-01T10:52:44+02:00",
