          "id": "91a24a9fb0b892938dfb6c121f604c68973a1ecb",
          "message": "feat: query plan caching (#29038)\n\n## Summary\n\nThis PR implements **query plan caching** for Prisma Client, effectively\neliminating the query compilation overhead that was introduced by the\narchitecture shift in Prisma 7.\n\n### Background\n\nIn Prisma 6, the query engine ran as a separate Rust process (or native\nlibrary addon), so query compilation and execution happened on a\nseparate thread and never blocked the JavaScript event loop. In Prisma\n7, the query compiler runs as a Wasm module on the main JavaScript\nthread and execution happens in JavaScript itself. While this eliminated\nthe IPC overhead and simplified the architecture, it meant that every\nquery now synchronously blocks the event loop during compilation\n(typically 0.1-1ms per query). Under load, this compilation overhead\nadds up and contends with other work on the event loop.\n\n### Key idea\n\nIn a typical application, query *shapes* are mostly static -- only the\nuser-provided *values* change between calls. For example:\n\n```ts\nprisma.user.findUnique({ where: { email: 'alice@prisma.io' } })\nprisma.user.findUnique({ where: { email: 'bob@prisma.io' } })\n```\n\nThese two queries have the same structure and produce the same query\nplan. Only the email value differs. If we can extract the varying values\nand normalize the query into a canonical shape, we can use that shape as\na cache key and skip recompilation entirely on subsequent calls.\n\n### Solution\n\nThe implementation introduces **schema-aware query parameterization**:\nbefore sending a query to the Wasm compiler, the runtime replaces\nuser-provided data values with typed placeholders (`%1`, `%2`, ...),\nproducing a stable cache key. On a cache hit, the previously compiled\nquery plan is reused and only the placeholder values are swapped in at\nexecution time.\n\nThe parameterization is *schema-aware* rather than heuristic-based. A\n**ParamGraph** (parameterization graph) is built at generator time from\nthe DMMF and embedded in the generated client. At runtime, the graph\ntells the parameterizer exactly which fields accept scalar values,\nenums, lists, or nested objects, and what types are expected, so it only\nparameterizes values it can safely replace.\n\n### Architecture\n\n```\nGenerator time:                    Runtime:\n\nDMMF ──> ParamGraph ──> serialize   deserialize ──> ParamGraph\n         (builder)      (binary)                    (runtime view)\n                            │                            │\n                            ▼                            ▼\n                     Generated Client            Parameterizer\n                                                      │\n                                              ┌───────┴────────┐\n                                              ▼                ▼\n                                         Cache Key       Placeholder\n                                        (normalized       Values\n                                          query)        (extracted)\n                                              │                │\n                                              ▼                ▼\n                                        LRU Cache ──> Query Plan ──> Executor\n```\n\n**Generator time:**\n1. The DMMF is traversed to build a `ParamGraph` -- a compact directed\ngraph describing which query arguments are parameterizable and what\ntypes they accept.\n2. The graph is serialized into a hybrid format: a string table (JSON\narray) + structural data (base64url-encoded binary with varint\nencoding). This keeps the embedded schema small.\n\n**Runtime (per query):**\n1. The `ParamGraph` is deserialized once at `PrismaClient` construction.\n2. For each query, the parameterizer walks the `JsonQuery` tree guided\nby the graph, replacing qualifying values with typed placeholders.\n3. The parameterized query is serialized to JSON and used as an LRU\ncache key.\n4. On a cache miss, the Wasm compiler compiles the query and the plan is\nstored.\n5. On a cache hit (the common case), compilation is skipped entirely.\n6. The executor receives the cached plan + the extracted placeholder\nvalues.\n\n### Impact\n\n- **~100% cache hit rate** in typical applications, since query shapes\nare mostly static.\n- **Compilation cost drops from 0.1-1ms to 1-10µs** per query on cache\nhits (just the parameterization + cache lookup).\n- Effectively eliminates the event loop contention issue introduced by\nthe Wasm architecture.\n- Both single queries and batched queries benefit from caching.\n\n### New packages\n\n- **`@prisma/param-graph`**: Runtime ParamGraph representation, binary\nserialization/deserialization, edge flags, and scalar masks.\n- **`@prisma/param-graph-builder`**: Generator-time construction of the\nParamGraph from DMMF. Includes iterative DMMF traversal (to avoid stack\noverflow on complex schemas) and conservative union type merging.\n\n### Changes by area\n\n**Query parameterization** (`packages/client/.../parameterization/`):\n- `classify.ts` -- Value classification (null, primitive, tagged scalar,\nstructural, array, object).\n- `parameterize.ts` -- Core parameterization algorithm. Schema-guided\ntree walk with placeholder deduplication (identical values share\nplaceholders so the query compiler can reason about value equality).\n\n**Query plan cache** (`packages/client/.../query-plan-cache.ts`):\n- LRU cache with separate maps for single and batch query plans (1000\nentries per map by default).\n\n**Client engine integration** (`ClientEngine.ts`):\n- Wires parameterization + cache lookup into the single-query and\nbatch-query request paths.\n- Passes `placeholderValues` through the execution chain to the\ninterpreter.\n\n**Batch decompaction** (`packages/client-engine-runtime/src/batch.ts`):\n- `convertCompactedRows` now resolves placeholders in compacted batch\narguments before matching rows to results.\n\n**Code generation** (`client-generator-js`, `client-generator-ts`):\n- Both generators build and embed the serialized ParamGraph in the\ngenerated client configuration.\n\n**JSON protocol** (`packages/json-protocol`):\n- New `Placeholder`, `PlaceholderType`, and `PlaceholderTaggedValue`\ntypes for representing parameterized values in the JSON wire format.\n\n### Test plan\n\n- [x] Unit tests for ParamGraph serialization round-trips (including\nvarint boundary values and optional field encoding)\n- [x] Unit tests for parameterization across all value types: scalars,\ntagged values (DateTime, Decimal, BigInt, Bytes, Json), enums, arrays,\nnested objects, structural values (FieldRef, Enum tags)\n- [x] Unit tests for filter operators (equals, gt, lt, gte, lte,\ncontains, startsWith, endsWith, in, notIn, not)\n- [x] Unit tests for placeholder naming determinism and deduplication\n- [x] Unit tests for cache key consistency (same shape -> same key,\ndifferent shape -> different key)\n- [x] Unit tests for batch parameterization with cross-query placeholder\nuniqueness\n- [x] Unit tests for LRU cache behavior (eviction, refresh on access,\ncapacity limits)\n- [x] Benchmarks for parameterization and caching overhead (CodSpeed\nintegration)\n- [x] Existing functional and integration test suites pass\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: Alberto Schiabel <jkomyno@users.noreply.github.com>",
