          "id": "3c4013c82f3a0ec9acd630a3781bdad5f792e78d",
          "message": "fix: harden ~/.config/prisma-platform/auth.json to 0o600 (#29568)\n\n## Summary\n\n`CredentialsStore.writeCredentialsToDisk` in\n`packages/credentials-store/src/index.ts` writes the Prisma Platform\nOAuth tokens (`token` + `refreshToken` per `workspaceId`) to the user's\nauth file via `writeFile(this.authFilePath, JSON.stringify(data, null,\n2))` with no `mode` option. Node's default 0o666 masked by umask 022\ngives 0o644 on macOS Terminal and most Linux defaults, so the file is\nworld-readable. The surrounding directory was likewise being created at\n0o755.\n\nAny local account or process that can traverse the home directory can\nrecover the token + refreshToken and call the Prisma Platform management\nAPI as the user.\n\n## Files changed\n\n- `packages/credentials-store/src/index.ts` — added `chmod` to the\nimports, passed `{ mode: 0o600 }` to `writeFile` and `{ mode: 0o700 }`\nto `mkdir`, plus a follow-up `chmod` for both file and directory\n(wrapped in `try/catch` for Windows) to handle the pre-existing-file\ncase.\n\n## How to verify\n\n```js\nconst { writeFile, mkdir } = require('node:fs/promises');\nconst path = require('path');\nconst fs = require('fs');\n\n(async () => {\n    const p = '/tmp/prisma-perm-test/auth.json';\n    try { fs.unlinkSync(p); } catch {}\n    try { fs.rmdirSync(path.dirname(p)); } catch {}\n    await mkdir(path.dirname(p), { recursive: true });\n    await writeFile(p, JSON.stringify({tokens:[{workspaceId:'x',token:'y'}]}, null, 2));\n    console.log('file mode (pre-PR):', (fs.statSync(p).mode & 0o777).toString(8));\n})();\n```\n\nPre-PR: `file mode (pre-PR): 644`. After applying the PR: `file mode:\n600`.\n\n## Industry baseline\n\nGitHub CLI (`~/.config/gh/hosts.yml`), AWS CLI (`~/.aws/credentials`),\nGoogle Cloud SDK (`~/.config/gcloud/credentials.db`), Stripe CLI,\nPlanetScale CLI (keyring + 0o600 fallback), Pulumi (`StoreCredentials`\nuses `lockedfile.Write` at 0o600). The class docstring at line 19 even\nnotes \"this class might be adapted to store and retrieve credentials\nfrom other locations. E.g. keychains\" — until the keychain backend\nships, the on-disk store needs to apply the standard credential-file\nmode.\n\n## Related disclosure\n\nThis PR is the fix for the issue reported privately via\nGHSA-cgq2-43wx-qgpq. Filing both the GHSA and this PR so the maintainer\nteam has both the disclosure trail and the ready-to-merge fix.",
