          "id": "93da6ea2dcd65b8b4b9d58daf2e2821f1a4e9320",
          "message": "ci(e2e): shard client e2e suite across 3 jobs to fix chronic timeouts (#29619)\n\n## Problem\n\nThe `client-e2e` job chronically hit `timeout-minutes` and got cancelled\n— the ~90 Docker-based e2e tests (each a fresh `pnpm install`) don't\nfinish within one job at `--maxWorkers 3` (it was already bumped 25 → 35\nin `e8b5825fe` and still timed out).\n\n## Part 1 — shard the suite\n\n- `tests/e2e/_utils/run.ts`: add `--shard <index>/<count>` (sorted,\nround-robin → stable, disjoint, balanced; exact `^(\\d+)\\/(\\d+)$`\nvalidation).\n- `test-template.yml`: run `client-e2e` as a 3-way matrix (`1/3`, `2/3`,\n`3/3`).\n\nEach shard finishes in ~16–22 min, well under timeout.\n\n## Part 2 — fix the failures sharding exposed\n\nLetting the suite finish surfaced failures that were pre-existing on\nmain (the timeout was hiding them):\n\n1. **Node version.** The 8 `_steps.cts` files import ESM-only `zx`; as\nCommonJS that's `require('zx')`, which throws\n`ERR_VM_MODULE_LINK_FAILURE` on Node 20. `custom-output-yarn` fails\nbecause `@prisma/streams-local` needs Node `>=22`. → Bump the e2e Docker\nimage `node:20.19 → node:22`.\n2. **Remove the dead `generator-build` shim.**\n`prisma-client-imports-sqlite` ENOENT'd on\n`@prisma/client/generator-build`'s sibling\n`prisma_schema_build_bg.wasm`. That entrypoint\n(`src/generation/generator.ts`) is a JSON-RPC adapter for the\nprisma-client-js generator that, per its own doc comment, *\"is only used\nby Studio … will be removed\"*. Prisma 7's Studio no longer uses it, it's\nnever been user-facing, and it had silently been broken for a while — so\nrather than ship a 2.9 MB wasm to keep it alive, **remove it entirely**:\nthe entrypoint, its esbuild config, the `./generator-build` export +\n`files` entry, the jest ignore, and the e2e import-assertions.\n3. **Run the ESM `_steps` files as `.ts`.** The `_steps` files are ESM\nbut were executed as `.cts`, so `import { $ } from 'zx'` (ESM-only) blew\nup. Run them as `.ts`, and since a named import of the\nCommonJS-transpiled `executeSteps` helper isn't statically resolvable\nunder ESM, import the module default and destructure `executeSteps` from\nit. Exclude `_steps.ts` (alongside `_steps.cts`) from the fixture\ntsconfigs so the steps are run directly by the runtime rather than\ntype-compiled.\n\n## Part 3 — bugs the green suite caught / runner portability\n\n1. **SQL commenter leaked user data (product bug).** For non-raw\nclient-engine queries, `ClientEngine` built the `SqlCommenterQueryInfo`\nfrom the original query (single, batched, and compacted paths), so\nplugins such as `query-insights` received real user data values instead\nof `Param` placeholders. Pass the **parameterized** query payloads into\nthe SQL commenter context so user data never reaches the SQL comments.\nThe `query-insights` e2e assertions are hardened to check for the\n`Param` placeholder shape instead of exact JSON equality.\n2. **Make the Docker runner work on SELinux-enabled Linux hosts.**\nBind-mounted e2e files were unreadable inside the container on SELinux\nhosts, and `chmod +x`-then-exec of the mounted startup script could\nfail. Append the `:z` relabel option to bind volumes on Linux, invoke\nthe startup script through `bash` instead of relying on the executable\nbit, and tag the built image (`prisma-client-test-e2e`) so it can be\nreused.\n\n## ⚠️ Required status checks\n\nSharding renames the e2e check to `Client E2E … (shard 1/3 | 2/3 |\n3/3)`. If branch protection requires the old `🧪 / Client E2E\nubuntu-latest [v20.19.0]` by name, repoint it to the three shard checks\non merge.\n\n## Notes\n\n- e2e no longer runs on the minimum supported Node 20.19;\nfunctional/unit suites still cover Node 20 (and\n`@prisma/streams-local`'s `>=22` engine, defined in another repo, makes\na Node-20-green e2e unattainable from here anyway).\n- A stale `// TODO … @prisma/client/generator-build …` comment remains\nin `internals/resolveBinary.ts` (a historical engine-path note, not\ncode); left out of scope.\n\n---------\n\nSigned-off-by: Alexey Orlenko's AI Agent <robot@aqrln.net>",
