          "id": "4da377494cc27bc0784d3629df21e94989abd153",
          "message": "fix(client): reject invalid raw dates (#29697)\n\nSummary\nThis PR fixes a bug in raw query parameter serialization where an\ninvalid Date passed to $queryRaw or $executeRaw could be silently\nconverted into the string \"null\" instead of causing a validation error.\nThat behavior is dangerous because it can corrupt the value that gets\nsent to the database without the user realizing it. The fix makes raw\nparameter handling consistent with the structured query path, which\nalready rejects invalid Date objects.\nProblem\nIn serializeRawParameters, Date values were serialized with\nparameter.toJSON().\nFor a valid Date, this works as expected and produces an ISO timestamp.\nFor an invalid Date, however, JavaScript’s Date.prototype.toJSON()\nreturns null by spec. That means an invalid input like:\nnew Date('not a date')\nwas being serialized as:\n{ \"prisma__type\": \"date\", \"prisma__value\": null }\nAfter deserialization, this could end up being treated like the literal\nstring \"null\" and then bound as a datetime value. That is a silent\nfailure mode, and it is inconsistent with the normal Prisma client\nargument validation behavior.\nExpected behavior\nInvalid Date values should not be serialized for raw queries.\nInstead, Prisma should fail fast with a clear validation error so users\nimmediately know the input is invalid.\nFix\nThis PR updates\n[`packages/client/src/runtime/utils/serializeRawParameters.ts`](/d:/coding/pisma/prisma/packages/client/src/runtime/utils/serializeRawParameters.ts)\nto validate date values before serialization using the existing\nisValidDate() helper.\nIf the value is a Date but it is invalid, the serializer now throws:\nInvalid value for argument `date`: Provided Date object is invalid.\nExpected Date.\nThis keeps raw parameter serialization aligned with the structured query\npath and prevents invalid date values from being silently transformed\ninto misleading raw payloads.\nWhy this is the right behavior\nIt preserves data integrity by rejecting bad input instead of silently\nchanging it.\nIt makes raw query handling consistent with Prisma’s existing validation\nbehavior.\nIt gives the user an actionable error message immediately, instead of\nletting the invalid value travel deeper into query execution.\nIt avoids confusing cases where \"null\" appears to be a valid serialized\nvalue even though the original input was an invalid date.\nRegression test\nI added a test in\n[`packages/client/src/__tests__/serializeRawParameters.test.ts`](/d:/coding/pisma/prisma/packages/client/src/__tests__/serializeRawParameters.test.ts)\nthat verifies invalid dates throw during raw parameter serialization.\nThis ensures the bug does not regress in future changes.\nNotes\nThis change is intentionally small and isolated to raw parameter\nserialization.\nThe fix does not change behavior for valid dates, BigInts, decimals,\nbuffers, or other supported parameter types.\nI could not run the full Vitest suite in this environment because\ndependencies are not installed here.",
