          "distinct": true,
          "id": "b82985029d44349849dfbb4990bb19278178ee61",
          "message": "ci: upgrade pnpm to 11 to restore pnpm audit (#29725)\n\n## Problem\n\n`pnpm audit --prod` fails in CI:\n\n```\nERR_PNPM_AUDIT_BAD_RESPONSE  The audit endpoint (at\nhttps://registry.npmjs.org/-/npm/v1/security/audits) responded with 410:\n{\"error\":\"This endpoint is being retired. Use the bulk advisory endpoint instead.\"}\n```\n\nnpm retired the legacy audit endpoint (its brownout ended 2026-07-15),\nwhich now\nreturns HTTP 410. pnpm 10.x calls that endpoint. The fix\n([pnpm#11268](https://github.com/pnpm/pnpm/pull/11268))\nships only in **pnpm 11**, which uses the bulk advisory endpoint — there\nis no\npnpm 10.x release that works, so this is a package-manager upgrade\nrather than a bump.\n\n## Solution\n\nUpgrade the repo to **pnpm 11.13.1** (`packageManager`, `engines.pnpm`).\n\n### Keeping Node.js 20 test coverage\n\npnpm 11 requires **Node.js ≥ 22.13**, but the CI matrix still exercises\nNode.js 20\n(and 22.12). Rather than drop that coverage, every `pnpm/action-setup`\nstep now sets\n**`standalone: true`**, which installs pnpm as a self-contained\nexecutable\n(`@pnpm/exe`) that bundles its own Node.js. pnpm then runs on its\nbundled Node.js,\nwhile `pnpm run <script>` — install, build, and every test — executes\nunder the\nmatrix Node.js resolved from `PATH`. This was verified empirically:\nscripts spawned\nby the standalone pnpm use the `PATH` `node`, not pnpm's bundled one, so\na Node.js 20\njob genuinely tests on Node.js 20.\n\n### Config migration (pnpm 11 no longer reads the old locations)\n\n- Dependency `overrides` moved from `package.json` (`pnpm.overrides`) to\n`pnpm-workspace.yaml`.\n- `.npmrc` pnpm settings moved to `pnpm-workspace.yaml` in camelCase\n(`shellEmulator`,\n`autoInstallPeers`, `savePrefix`); `.npmrc` is removed (pnpm 11 reads\nonly auth/registry from it).\n- **Build-script approval: `onlyBuiltDependencies` → `allowBuilds`.**\npnpm 11 removed\n`onlyBuiltDependencies` and replaced it with `allowBuilds` (a package →\n`true`/`false` map).\nIt also defaults `strictDepBuilds` to `true`, so an unreviewed\ndependency build script now\n**fails `pnpm install` with `ERR_PNPM_IGNORED_BUILDS` (exit 1)** — a\nwarning-only situation\nunder pnpm 10. The native-build allow-list (`@swc/core`,\n`better-sqlite3`, `esbuild`, `sharp`,\n  `sqlite3`, `workerd`, `yarn`) is converted to `allowBuilds`.\n- The e2e Docker container (`standard.dockerfile`) **deliberately stays\non pnpm 10.x**: its\nstandalone consumer projects rely on per-test `package.json`\n`pnpm.overrides`, which pnpm 11\n  ignores. Its comment is updated to say so.\n- **Bundle-size `prisma` invocation.** pnpm 11 doesn't create a\nworkspace package's `node_modules/.bin`\nentry when the bin target is missing at install time (`prisma`'s\n`build/index.js` is built later)\nand never backfills it, so the bundle-size script's `pnpm prisma\ngenerate` failed with\n`ERR_PNPM_RECURSIVE_EXEC_FIRST_FAIL: Command \"prisma\" not found`.\n`create-gzip-files.ts` now invokes\nthe Prisma CLI through its resolved built entry (`node\n…/prisma/build/index.js`) instead of the bin.\n\n## Verification (against pnpm 11.13.1)\n\n- `pnpm install --frozen-lockfile` → exit 0. **`pnpm-lock.yaml` is\nunchanged**: pnpm 11 keeps\n`lockfileVersion 9.0` and accepts the existing resolution, so there is\nno lockfile churn.\n- `pnpm audit --prod` → `No known vulnerabilities found`, exit 0. The\n410 is gone.\n- `pnpm approve-builds` → `There are no packages awaiting approval`:\n`allowBuilds` covers every\nbuild-script dependency, so `strictDepBuilds` has nothing to reject. A\nreal `CI=true pnpm install`\nruns the native build scripts (better-sqlite3, sqlite3, …) instead of\nignoring them.\n\n## Notes / out of scope\n\n- `engines.node` is intentionally unchanged — Node.js 20 remains\nsupported.\n- CLI test fixtures that embed `pnpm@10.15.1` as detection input, and\nthe `prisma-init-corepack`\ne2e fixture, are left as-is (they are part of the pnpm-10 e2e world /\narbitrary test data).\n\nSigned-off-by: Alexey Orlenko's AI Agent <robot@aqrln.net>",
