          "distinct": true,
          "id": "a0179ce3ba5c8c38053ef7ed44af472d851fd933",
          "message": "perf(build): build bundles in parallel and emit types once per package (#28028)\n\nBuilds the bundles of a package concurrently, and emits each package's\ntypes once instead of once per bundle.\n\n## Changes\n\n- **Type emission** (`helpers/compile/plugins/tscPlugin.ts`): the plugin\nruns per bundle, but its work only ever depended on the package. Every\nbundle compiled the same tsconfig into the same `outDir`, and bundles\nsharing an `outfile` ran API Extractor over the same inputs to the same\npath. A small `once` helper shares one promise per tsconfig and per\nbundle path, so each happens once. The cjs bundle's copy step is also\ngone: API Extractor writes `<outfile>.d.ts` itself, so that\nread-and-write-back was copying a file onto itself.\n\n- **Bundle execution** (`helpers/compile/build.ts`): `build()` now runs\nits bundles through `Promise.all` rather than `transduce.async`, and\nlogs a completion line with an `n/total` counter, since with concurrent\nbuilds the start lines interleave and no longer say what finished.\n\n## Why\n\nEmitting types once is what makes the concurrency safe, so the two\nhalves belong together. Bundles that share an `outfile` — which is every\npackage on `bundledConfig`, and `runtime/client` in `@prisma/client` —\nboth write `<outfile>.d.ts`, and the esm bundle reads that same file\nback to produce `<outfile>.d.mts`. Running them at the same time races\non a single path, and the window scales with the size of the type\nbundle, so the largest packages are the most exposed. Deduplicating\nremoves the race by construction rather than by locking.\n\nIt is also where the speedup comes from. Measured on a 9-core machine\nwith `turbo run build --force`:\n\n| | full build |\n| --- | --- |\n| before | 280s |\n| after | 126s |\n\nParallelism alone accounts for very little of that. Turborepo already\nbuilds packages concurrently, so the cores are busy before this change;\non a full build, parallelising within a package measured 290s against\n289s — no movement. On a single package it is worth a few percent, which\nis a real if modest win for `pnpm --filter <pkg> build` in the inner\nloop. The rest is the removed duplicate work.\n\n## Verification\n\nAll 1337 emitted `.d.ts` / `.d.mts` files are byte-identical to the\nprevious output, compared by checksum across a full build of every\npackage.\n\nRebased onto `main`. The original diff called `dependencyCheck`, which\n#28024 removed along with `depCheckPlugin` when it introduced Turborepo;\nthat call and the now-unused `transduce` import are dropped.",
